{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook explains that high‑pressure compressor inspection is done by removing the inlet screen, cleaning the inducer, visually examining each vane with strong light (and a mirror when needed), turning the compressor to view all surfaces, and using magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant inspection for repaired blades.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "For centrifugal‑flow engines, the compressor inducer is inspected by first removing the air‑inlet screen, cleaning the inducer, and examining it with a strong light while slowly turning the compressor to check each vane for cracks, especially in the leading edges.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Repaired compressor blades are inspected using magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant inspection methods to ensure all damage traces have been removed.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspection of compressor components includes checking for cracks, dents, and foreign‑object damage, and any crack is generally cause for component rejection.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook describes visual and light‑aided inspection of compressor blades (including magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant methods) and states that blending may be used for minor, rounded damage that does not exceed the manufacturer‑specified limits and shows no cracks.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Compressor blades are inspected visually with a strong light and may be examined by magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant inspection to verify that all damage has been removed.",
      "citations": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Minor, well‑rounded damage to leading or trailing edges in the outer half of a blade can be repaired by blending, provided the indentation depth does not exceed the limits set by the engine manufacturer’s service and overhaul manuals and no cracks are present.",
      "citations": [2, 1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blending is performed by stoning out material beyond the damage depth, applying a generous radius at the blend edges, and smoothing the area with a crocus cloth to achieve a surface finish comparable to a new blade.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "For centrifugal‑flow engines, the compressor inducer vanes are inspected after removing the inlet screen, cleaned, and examined with a strong light while slowly turning the compressor to look for leading‑edge cracks.",
      "citations": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "All repair decisions must reference the specific manufacturer’s current information for allowable limits and procedures.",
      "citations": [3]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": "The passages repeatedly refer to the engine manufacturer’s service and overhaul instruction manuals for the exact allowable repair limits and procedures; those specific values are not provided in the handbook."
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "A high exhaust gas temperature indicates that the turbine section may be overheating, which can reduce component life and cause engine deterioration.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Excess EGT reduces turbine component life and can cause engine deterioration if the temperature exceeds allowable limits.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "A borescope is an inspection tool that allows visual examination of the interior of a turbine engine without disassembly.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "A borescope is an inspection tool for viewing the inside of a turbine engine without disassembling it; it consists of a light, mirror, and magnifying lens mounted inside a small‑diameter tube that is inserted through borescope inspection ports.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook describes that a hot‑section inspection checks the combustion case, combustion chambers, outlet ducts, turbine blades, nozzle guide vanes, turbine disc, turbine nozzle, fuel nozzle assemblies, and related components for cracks, overheating, wear, warping, foreign‑object damage, and corrosion.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Inspect the external combustion case for hot spots, exhaust leaks, and distortions before it is opened.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "After opening the case, inspect the combustion chambers for localized overheating, cracks, and excessive wear.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspect the combustion chamber outlet ducts and turbine nozzle for cracks and evidence of foreign‑object damage.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspect the first‑stage turbine blades and nozzle guide vanes for cracks, warping, or foreign‑object damage.",
      "citations": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Visually inspect turbine discs for cracks, using penetrant or other structural inspection techniques; any disc cracks require rejection.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspect turbine blades for stress‑rupture cracks, leading‑edge deformation, and other damage using strong light and magnification.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspect fuel nozzle assemblies for carbon deposits, nicks, burrs, and clean them with approved cleaning fluid and filtered air.",
      "citations": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Inspect combustion chamber liners, covers, and swirl vanes for cracks, pits, corrosion, and other damage, using visible dye or fluorescent penetrant methods.",
      "citations": [6]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": "The passages repeatedly state that evaluation limits, repair criteria, and detailed procedures must follow the specific engine manufacturer's current maintenance and overhaul manuals."
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook defines the high‑pressure compressor (N2) as the second‑stage compressor of a dual‑spool engine, forming the high‑pressure spool that supplies air to the core and is driven by the high‑pressure turbine, with its speed controlled by the fuel control.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The high‑pressure compressor, called the N2 compressor, is the second‑stage compressor in a dual‑spool gas‑turbine engine and is rotated by the starter for starting, with its rpm controlled by the fuel control.",
      "citations": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "In a dual‑spool engine the high‑pressure spool contains the high‑pressure compressor that supplies air to the combustion section of the engine.",
      "citations": [1]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "C‑MAPSS simula la degradación de los motores variando exponencialmente los parámetros de flujo y eficiencia de los módulos rotativos.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "La degradación se modela modificando los parámetros de flujo (f) y eficiencia (e) de los módulos del motor mediante una tasa exponencial de cambio, partiendo de valores iniciales aleatorios dentro de un pequeño rango de deterioro.",
      "citations": [1, 4, 6]
    },
    {
      "text": "Los modificadores de flujo y eficiencia para cada componente (Fan, LPC, HPC, HPT, LPT) se introducen como entradas al modelo y se ajustan gradualmente hasta que el índice de salud alcanza cero, que es el criterio de falla.",
      "citations": [2, 5]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": ""
}

